cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use HEPRE in academic work, please cite it as below."
title: "HEPRE: privacy-preserving clinical risk scoring under homomorphic encryption and proxy re-encryption"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Martin
    given-names: Alex
    email: hepre@capsula.by
repository-code: "https://github.com/datascienceadvice/hepre"
url: "https://github.com/datascienceadvice/hepre"
license: BSD-2-Clause
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-09-07
keywords:
  - homomorphic encryption
  - CKKS
  - proxy re-encryption
  - OpenFHE
  - Rust
  - clinical laboratory diagnostics
  - risk scoring
  - privacy-preserving machine learning
abstract: >-
  HEPRE is a research prototype and reproducibility package for
  privacy-preserving type-2-diabetes risk scoring under homomorphic encryption.
  The mathematical core is OpenFHE (CKKS for approximate real arithmetic, BFV
  for an integer control path, and lattice-based proxy re-encryption in the
  INDCPA-PRE mode), wrapped in Rust through a cxx bridge with a C++ FFI shim.
  Three role CLIs — laboratory, computing server and hospital — exchange only
  serialized ciphertexts and keys, which is how the prototype is implemented. The
  scenario evaluated in the accompanying manuscript is bilateral: a laboratory and
  an external assessor (e.g., an insurer), where the assessor's environment plays
  the computing role and the laboratory performs the proxy re-encryption of the
  result. A laboratory that owns the patient features standardizes and encrypts
  them and issues the proxy re-encryption key. A server evaluates the full
  logistic-regression score homomorphically -- a linear logit followed by a
  polynomial approximation of the sigmoid -- without ever seeing the features or
  holding any decryption key. A hospital decrypts only the transferred probability
  and applies the clinical threshold. The repository contains the wrapper API with
  RAII handles and persistent error handling at the FFI boundary, three role CLIs
  with a file-based handoff, a deterministic synthetic cohort generator with a
  documented ground-truth data-generating process, the homomorphic inference
  circuit, an in-repository statistics module, a study runner that regenerates
  every reported number, and an integration test suite. All results are shipped
  as machine-readable artifacts with SHA-256 digests and an environment
  fingerprint. It is a prototype intended for research and reproducibility only,
  and must not be used with real patient data.
